                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a363d21d-c628-32a8-ba8e-4155e8dae85d",
                        "entry_id": "3365f3c7-9f64-35c8-9cf9-9990d84a8af0",
                        "glosses": [
                            "палеонт., зоол. беспозвоночное животное класса головоногих моллюсков, распространённое с каменноугольного по меловой период и вымершее в конце мезозоя"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f7e8ba46-9632-3343-85af-6b84d0c4ad3e",
                        "entry_id": "3365f3c7-9f64-35c8-9cf9-9990d84a8af0",
                        "glosses": [
                            "палеонт., зоол. пальцеобразная часть окаменелого скелета такого животного; чёртов палец"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "0f1ca687-3652-46da-b61d-50790a0609b1",
                        "source_entry_id": "3365f3c7-9f64-35c8-9cf9-9990d84a8af0",
                        "source_sense_description": "моллюск",
                        "target_lang_word": "belemnite",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "belemnite",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0261b538-2e39-3901-83d1-54fe3354291c",
                                        "entry_id": "a422996b-d7a3-3732-b46b-f27271359ce7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any member of the extinct order †Belemnitida of Mesozoic marine cephalopods, very similar in many ways to the modern squid and closely related to the modern cuttlefish."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "85e4abb5-cedc-4e73-97bf-77544c5cf63a",
                                                "sense_id": "0261b538-2e39-3901-83d1-54fe3354291c",
                                                "text": "In the universal decay this volatile substance had chanced to survive, perhaps through many thousands of centuries. It reminded me of a sepia painting I had once seen done from the ink of a fossil Belemnite that must have perished and become fossilized millions of years ago."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b2587643-d317-465b-a5dd-9906152b03b7",
                                                "sense_id": "0261b538-2e39-3901-83d1-54fe3354291c",
                                                "text": "The guts of fossilized marine reptiles are packed with belemnites, and it's been theorized that some prehistoric sharks even died from the overconsumption of heavy belemnite shell guards."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "d31d5348-bee3-4f3b-8abe-657497ece1f0",
                        "source_entry_id": "3365f3c7-9f64-35c8-9cf9-9990d84a8af0",
                        "source_sense_description": "моллюск",
                        "target_lang_word": "devil's finger",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "devil's finger",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5dcbe09c-2302-3517-887d-8a5ae7050295",
                                        "entry_id": "b10aab24-32fd-3355-b7ef-6f057c5c2c62",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A belemnite, any of various extinct species of cephalopod in the order Belemnitida."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "baf2d2a5-4862-3b50-95db-18cd963eca27",
                                        "entry_id": "b10aab24-32fd-3355-b7ef-6f057c5c2c62",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The fossil remains of such a cephalopod."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7cd11ee8-9313-4f7b-90ca-174dceec17a4",
                                                "sense_id": "baf2d2a5-4862-3b50-95db-18cd963eca27",
                                                "text": "LXXVI. Belemnites, a conical or fusiform stone, of brown radiating spar, […] Various names have been assigned to this fossil; many of which, derived from the ridiculous notions entertained by the vulgar, in the early ages, need only be mentioned. Such are, devil’s fingers, Spectrorum candela, and idæus dactylus, from their having somewhat the form of fingers"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2312b07b-7429-4b50-8479-c8a03facfd98",
                                                "sense_id": "baf2d2a5-4862-3b50-95db-18cd963eca27",
                                                "text": "In the Middle Ages, when fields in the Norfolk chalk and Oxford clay outcrops of England were ploughed, they would appear after a thunderstorm. The myth soon emerged that they were thunderbolts. It is more likely that the heavy downpour had washed away the surrounding soil to reveal the fossils. Belemnites are also known as St Peter’s fingers, devil’s fingers and ghostly candles."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3da936db-c9af-4ff2-9394-5ddff5254260",
                                                "sense_id": "baf2d2a5-4862-3b50-95db-18cd963eca27",
                                                "text": "Bivalves, a type of Jurassic oyster, are known as ‘devil’s toenails’ and the bullet shaped belemnites, which look like dinosaur teeth and are the remains of a creature resembling a cuttlefish, are, locally, called ‘devil’s fingers’."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "29e81427-3949-4a97-812a-781160544f80",
                        "source_entry_id": "3365f3c7-9f64-35c8-9cf9-9990d84a8af0",
